1
00:00:55,000 --> 00:00:59,000
"The Last Oath"

2
00:00:59,600 --> 00:01:04,100
(Episode 16: Treatment Log)

3
00:01:16,833 --> 00:01:17,833
Where did you go?

4
00:01:18,291 --> 00:01:19,434
I just went to the cafeteria.

5
00:01:19,458 --> 00:01:21,258
Figured they'd run
out of food if I was late.

6
00:01:21,833 --> 00:01:23,166
It's fine. I'm not hungry.

7
00:01:23,375 --> 00:01:24,895
Take it to Grandma Qiu and the others.

8
00:01:28,041 --> 00:01:29,125
Grandma Qiu!

9
00:01:31,416 --> 00:01:33,000
He Yao, what are you doing?!

10
00:01:38,291 --> 00:01:39,166
Stop fighting!

11
00:01:39,167 --> 00:01:40,333
A-Leh, stop it!

12
00:01:44,041 --> 00:01:45,041
Have you lost your mind?!

13
00:01:45,625 --> 00:01:47,416
Doctor He is treating Grandma Qiu's injury!

14
00:01:50,500 --> 00:01:51,541
You lying fraud!

15
00:01:52,166 --> 00:01:53,526
How dare you show your face here?!

16
00:01:54,000 --> 00:01:55,000
You two...

17
00:01:55,375 --> 00:01:56,375
Fools!

18
00:01:57,416 --> 00:01:58,708
You two utter knuckleheads!

19
00:01:59,500 --> 00:02:00,916
Doctor He was helping me!

20
00:02:05,666 --> 00:02:07,375
I was moving some boxes earlier and...

21
00:02:07,563 --> 00:02:09,063
I dislocated my shoulder.

22
00:02:09,166 --> 00:02:10,208
It hurt like hell.

23
00:02:10,500 --> 00:02:12,340
Doctor He was just
popping it back into place!

24
00:02:13,458 --> 00:02:13,791
And you two...

25
00:02:14,333 --> 00:02:16,708
How could you just jump
him without asking questions?

26
00:02:19,583 --> 00:02:20,583
I'm fine.

27
00:02:20,791 --> 00:02:22,910
Don't worry, everyone.
It's just a misunderstanding.

28
00:02:25,708 --> 00:02:26,708
Grandma Qiu,

29
00:02:27,125 --> 00:02:27,708
give it a try.

30
00:02:28,083 --> 00:02:29,323
Can you move your shoulder now?

31
00:02:33,541 --> 00:02:34,541
Yes.

32
00:02:34,916 --> 00:02:35,916
It's back in place!

33
00:02:36,125 --> 00:02:37,166
Thank you, Doctor He.

34
00:02:37,750 --> 00:02:38,750
Don't mention it.

35
00:02:38,916 --> 00:02:39,916
The two of them and I...

36
00:02:39,958 --> 00:02:41,358
just had a slight misunderstanding.

37
00:02:41,625 --> 00:02:42,625
Gentlemen,

38
00:02:43,166 --> 00:02:44,333
let's have a word over there.

39
00:02:49,083 --> 00:02:50,083
No fighting, now.

40
00:02:50,375 --> 00:02:51,455
Talk things out peacefully!

41
00:02:52,083 --> 00:02:53,083
Don't worry, Grandma.

42
00:03:05,458 --> 00:03:06,498
Why did you steal our car?

43
00:03:07,666 --> 00:03:08,707
It was an emergency.

44
00:03:08,708 --> 00:03:09,416
I had to leave.

45
00:03:09,417 --> 00:03:10,937
And I didn't want to drag you into it.

46
00:03:17,000 --> 00:03:18,000
Look,

47
00:03:18,250 --> 00:03:19,125
I admit that

48
00:03:19,126 --> 00:03:20,291
what I did was wrong.

49
00:03:23,333 --> 00:03:23,666
Lin Zai,

50
00:03:24,208 --> 00:03:25,208
I'm sorry.

51
00:03:25,333 --> 00:03:26,809
But I never meant to hurt either of you.

52
00:03:26,833 --> 00:03:28,125
Then what are you doing here?

53
00:03:28,791 --> 00:03:30,431
Don't tell me this is a coincidence, too.

54
00:03:32,083 --> 00:03:33,163
I couldn't find my brother.

55
00:03:34,041 --> 00:03:34,958
I drove out here,

56
00:03:34,959 --> 00:03:36,319
ran into Brother Dao and his crew,

57
00:03:36,583 --> 00:03:37,749
and stayed at this camp for the time being.

58
00:03:37,750 --> 00:03:38,750
Where's the car?

59
00:03:39,750 --> 00:03:40,750
It's parked out back.

60
00:03:40,916 --> 00:03:41,541
I'll take you to it.

61
00:03:41,916 --> 00:03:43,101
Can we just put this behind us?

62
00:03:43,125 --> 00:03:43,625
Fair enough?

63
00:03:43,875 --> 00:03:45,291
Whether we move on is my call.

64
00:03:55,166 --> 00:03:56,166
When did you get hurt?

65
00:03:57,041 --> 00:03:58,125
It's fine. Just a scratch.

66
00:03:59,750 --> 00:04:00,125
Hold still.

67
00:04:00,500 --> 00:04:01,500
The wound is this deep,

68
00:04:01,625 --> 00:04:02,665
and you didn't say a word?

69
00:04:07,416 --> 00:04:09,291
Take us to the car. Now.

70
00:04:12,666 --> 00:04:14,186
What tricks are you pulling this time?

71
00:04:16,875 --> 00:04:17,875
I'll take you there.

72
00:04:43,500 --> 00:04:44,700
Returning what belongs to you.

73
00:04:48,458 --> 00:04:49,458
It's out of gas.

74
00:05:06,875 --> 00:05:07,916
I didn't even realize

75
00:05:08,125 --> 00:05:09,500
there was a can of gas here.

76
00:05:12,958 --> 00:05:14,438
This must be the camp's reserve fuel.

77
00:05:14,750 --> 00:05:15,990
It's considered a vital supply.

78
00:05:16,750 --> 00:05:18,190
We can't just take it for ourselves.

79
00:05:19,333 --> 00:05:20,333
Lin Zai's right.

80
00:05:20,583 --> 00:05:21,875
It belongs to them, after all.

81
00:05:23,250 --> 00:05:24,708
Did anyone ask for your two cents?

82
00:05:30,541 --> 00:05:31,708
A-Leh, watch your tone.

83
00:05:33,250 --> 00:05:36,233
The fuel belongs to someone else.
Besides, we're in no rush to leave.

84
00:05:39,375 --> 00:05:40,375
Are we in no rush,

85
00:05:41,500 --> 00:05:43,140
or do you just not want to leave with me?

86
00:05:44,916 --> 00:05:46,076
What's that supposed to mean?

87
00:05:47,541 --> 00:05:48,581
If you're in such a hurry,

88
00:05:48,875 --> 00:05:49,916
then leave by yourself.

89
00:05:52,250 --> 00:05:53,250
Lin Zai!

90
00:06:14,250 --> 00:06:15,250
Changhuan,

91
00:06:15,291 --> 00:06:16,291
I met someone.

92
00:06:16,333 --> 00:06:17,083
He's just like you.

93
00:06:17,333 --> 00:06:18,933
His wounds heal at an astonishing speed,

94
00:06:18,958 --> 00:06:20,518
yet he shows no signs of zombification.

95
00:06:21,166 --> 00:06:22,166
I suspect

96
00:06:22,583 --> 00:06:24,207
this is an anomalous evolution

97
00:06:24,208 --> 00:06:25,888
resulting from resisting the zombie virus.

98
00:06:26,583 --> 00:06:28,263
I'll do everything I can to keep him here.

99
00:06:28,500 --> 00:06:29,958
If he really is an immune subject,

100
00:06:31,208 --> 00:06:32,208
our experiment

101
00:06:32,625 --> 00:06:33,625
is bound to succeed.

102
00:06:46,958 --> 00:06:47,958
You three...

103
00:06:48,250 --> 00:06:50,375
didn't cause any more
trouble just now, did you?

104
00:06:51,708 --> 00:06:52,791
Doctor He...

105
00:06:53,291 --> 00:06:55,458
He really doesn't seem
like a bad person to me.

106
00:06:58,250 --> 00:06:59,650
We cleared up the misunderstanding.

107
00:06:59,875 --> 00:07:00,875
Everything's fine.

108
00:07:01,583 --> 00:07:02,583
Doctor He...

109
00:07:02,584 --> 00:07:03,958
can be a bit of a loner,

110
00:07:04,208 --> 00:07:05,416
but he's a decent man.

111
00:07:05,833 --> 00:07:07,125
You youngsters...

112
00:07:16,458 --> 00:07:17,458
You're pressing too hard.

113
00:07:17,500 --> 00:07:18,500
Don't hurt Grandma.

114
00:07:19,541 --> 00:07:20,708
Alright, alright.

115
00:07:21,583 --> 00:07:23,303
It feels much better once it's loosened up.

116
00:07:23,375 --> 00:07:24,624
You must be exhausted, too.

117
00:07:24,625 --> 00:07:26,208
Go on back and get some rest.

118
00:07:27,541 --> 00:07:28,541
Don't worry, Grandma.

119
00:07:28,875 --> 00:07:29,875
I'll walk you back.

120
00:07:29,958 --> 00:07:30,958
Let's go.

121
00:09:03,708 --> 00:09:05,083
We got lucky today!

122
00:09:05,916 --> 00:09:07,500
Hauled in some fresh catch!

123
00:09:08,166 --> 00:09:09,875
Clean them up in a bit,

124
00:09:10,125 --> 00:09:12,625
and we'll add some fresh
flavor to tonight's dinner!

125
00:09:13,250 --> 00:09:14,250
See that?

126
00:09:17,000 --> 00:09:18,833
Awesome! Haven't had fresh meat in forever.

127
00:09:20,000 --> 00:09:21,000
Excuse me, sir.

128
00:09:21,916 --> 00:09:23,541
Could I have this sand crab?

129
00:09:23,958 --> 00:09:24,958
This one?

130
00:09:25,083 --> 00:09:25,583
Yeah.

131
00:09:25,916 --> 00:09:27,582
It's tiny, barely has any meat on it.

132
00:09:27,583 --> 00:09:28,833
What do you want it for?

133
00:09:29,708 --> 00:09:30,708
Can I have it, please?

134
00:09:32,333 --> 00:09:33,333
Just let him have it.

135
00:09:34,083 --> 00:09:35,125
Fine, it's yours.

136
00:09:40,958 --> 00:09:41,833
In a little while,

137
00:09:41,834 --> 00:09:43,416
I'll prep these and stew a pot of soup.

138
00:09:43,791 --> 00:09:46,000
We're eating good tonight!

139
00:09:46,125 --> 00:09:47,958
- Sound good? - Yeah!

140
00:09:57,125 --> 00:09:58,125
Lin Zai.

141
00:09:59,416 --> 00:10:00,416
This is for you.

142
00:10:01,833 --> 00:10:02,875
I remember you like these.

143
00:10:04,916 --> 00:10:05,916
I don't want it.

144
00:10:06,000 --> 00:10:07,000
Take it back.

145
00:10:20,291 --> 00:10:21,291
It's a gift.

146
00:10:21,666 --> 00:10:23,125
The apocalypse took so much from me.

147
00:10:24,291 --> 00:10:26,101
That's why I want to
cherish the people I find,

148
00:10:26,125 --> 00:10:27,125
the ones we can lean on.

149
00:10:27,833 --> 00:10:28,416
I feel like...

150
00:10:28,750 --> 00:10:30,583
we can be companions, like family.

151
00:10:31,750 --> 00:10:32,916
I'm used to being abandoned.

152
00:10:34,458 --> 00:10:36,250
Sacrificing others to save your own skin...

153
00:10:37,083 --> 00:10:38,583
isn't that just human nature?

154
00:10:39,083 --> 00:10:40,083
I'm not like them.

155
00:10:40,916 --> 00:10:42,125
My heart isn't made of stone.

156
00:10:48,291 --> 00:10:49,291
I'd lay down my life

157
00:10:51,458 --> 00:10:52,458
to protect you.

158
00:11:11,916 --> 00:11:12,916
Thank you.

159
00:11:13,208 --> 00:11:14,208
Go on back.

160
00:11:15,625 --> 00:11:17,041
So... are you still mad at me?

161
00:11:20,958 --> 00:11:21,958
I'm not mad at you.

162
00:11:23,083 --> 00:11:23,958
Then why are you—

163
00:11:23,959 --> 00:11:25,119
Too much has happened lately.

164
00:11:25,625 --> 00:11:26,625
My head's a mess.

165
00:11:27,750 --> 00:11:29,510
Just give me some
space to think it through.

166
00:11:30,250 --> 00:11:31,250
You go ahead first.

167
00:12:03,625 --> 00:12:04,625
Come in.

168
00:12:07,583 --> 00:12:08,915
I just brewed some herbs

169
00:12:08,916 --> 00:12:10,083
to take over to Grandma Qiu.

170
00:12:10,250 --> 00:12:11,770
Stopped by to check on you on the way.

171
00:12:13,416 --> 00:12:14,416
Got yourself a pet?

172
00:12:16,375 --> 00:12:17,375
Found it outside.

173
00:12:21,291 --> 00:12:22,291
A sand crab.

174
00:12:23,583 --> 00:12:24,625
Quite lively.

175
00:12:25,333 --> 00:12:26,333
Yeah.

176
00:12:26,583 --> 00:12:27,707
In a world like this,

177
00:12:27,708 --> 00:12:29,333
I'll keep it alive one day at a time.

178
00:12:30,416 --> 00:12:31,000
By the way,

179
00:12:31,208 --> 00:12:32,582
weren't you curious

180
00:12:32,583 --> 00:12:33,623
about my research earlier?

181
00:12:33,875 --> 00:12:35,642
I asked Brother Dao to
scrounge up some equipment

182
00:12:35,666 --> 00:12:36,986
and put together a makeshift lab.

183
00:12:37,166 --> 00:12:38,166
Want to go check it out?

184
00:12:39,583 --> 00:12:40,250
Right now?

185
00:12:40,500 --> 00:12:42,083
Yeah, if you're free.

186
00:12:42,166 --> 00:12:43,625
It's not far. Right next door.

187
00:12:46,666 --> 00:12:47,666
Sure.

188
00:12:58,291 --> 00:12:59,291
Come on in.

189
00:13:14,166 --> 00:13:16,541
All of this is your lab equipment?

190
00:13:17,666 --> 00:13:18,666
Cobbled together.

191
00:13:18,791 --> 00:13:19,791
Most of it was salvaged by Brother Dao

192
00:13:19,792 --> 00:13:21,708
and his men from various medical clinics.

193
00:13:22,125 --> 00:13:23,125
It's a mismatched mess,

194
00:13:23,166 --> 00:13:24,526
but at least it gets the job done.

195
00:13:26,500 --> 00:13:27,958
What's in here?

196
00:13:38,250 --> 00:13:38,708
Don't be afraid.

197
00:13:39,250 --> 00:13:40,374
It's a test subject I had Brother Dao

198
00:13:40,375 --> 00:13:41,458
help capture.

199
00:13:42,458 --> 00:13:43,625
He can even pull that off?

200
00:13:44,875 --> 00:13:46,515
Vaccine research is for the greater good.

201
00:13:46,583 --> 00:13:47,663
Of course he'd lend a hand.

202
00:14:27,666 --> 00:14:28,666
Brother...

203
00:14:39,583 --> 00:14:41,125
This is my brother, He Yao.

204
00:14:41,833 --> 00:14:42,833
Th-this...

205
00:14:43,166 --> 00:14:44,166
This is your brother?

206
00:14:44,583 --> 00:14:45,166
Are you sure?

207
00:14:45,250 --> 00:14:45,541
Yes.

208
00:14:46,000 --> 00:14:47,083
Yes, it's him.

209
00:14:52,541 --> 00:14:53,821
What are you going to do to him?

210
00:14:55,916 --> 00:14:57,166
If this were Casia,

211
00:14:57,625 --> 00:14:59,505
they'd drain his blood
and cerebrospinal fluid,

212
00:14:59,916 --> 00:15:01,332
harvest tissues or organs

213
00:15:01,333 --> 00:15:02,333
for in vitro cultivation,

214
00:15:02,791 --> 00:15:03,291
or...

215
00:15:03,291 --> 00:15:04,291
Enough!

216
00:15:04,583 --> 00:15:05,958
I won't let you do that to him!

217
00:15:08,625 --> 00:15:09,625
Don't panic.

218
00:15:09,916 --> 00:15:10,916
What I just described

219
00:15:11,083 --> 00:15:12,500
is Casia's standard protocol.

220
00:15:12,791 --> 00:15:13,791
But I...

221
00:15:14,000 --> 00:15:15,000
would never do that.

222
00:15:16,666 --> 00:15:17,666
What do you mean?

223
00:15:19,166 --> 00:15:20,333
What I truly want to develop

224
00:15:20,500 --> 00:15:21,500
was never a vaccine.

225
00:15:21,666 --> 00:15:23,250
It's an antiretroviral drug—

226
00:15:23,500 --> 00:15:25,220
a cure that turns zombies back into humans.

227
00:15:26,541 --> 00:15:27,000
What?

228
00:15:27,291 --> 00:15:28,291
Say that again.

229
00:15:31,458 --> 00:15:32,499
My line of research

230
00:15:32,500 --> 00:15:33,790
ran directly counter

231
00:15:33,791 --> 00:15:34,911
to Casia's upper management.

232
00:15:35,166 --> 00:15:36,249
They believed

233
00:15:36,250 --> 00:15:37,415
pursuing a zombie cure

234
00:15:37,416 --> 00:15:38,957
would blur the boundaries

235
00:15:38,958 --> 00:15:39,958
of a zombie's identity,

236
00:15:40,166 --> 00:15:42,125
triggering an
uncontrollable moral collapse.

237
00:15:43,791 --> 00:15:44,791
So,

238
00:15:45,000 --> 00:15:46,291
they banned my research

239
00:15:46,958 --> 00:15:48,278
and placed me under surveillance.

240
00:15:49,166 --> 00:15:50,166
That's why I fled.

241
00:15:56,166 --> 00:15:57,166
Simply put,

242
00:15:57,291 --> 00:15:58,708
Casia doesn't see zombies as human.

243
00:15:59,916 --> 00:16:00,916
But I do.

244
00:16:03,708 --> 00:16:04,708
Come with me.

245
00:16:43,875 --> 00:16:44,875
Is this for real?

246
00:16:46,166 --> 00:16:47,166
You can make a cure?

247
00:16:48,750 --> 00:16:49,208
No,

248
00:16:49,541 --> 00:16:50,541
I can't.

249
00:16:50,708 --> 00:16:51,708
At least, not yet.

250
00:16:53,250 --> 00:16:53,833
Why?

251
00:16:54,166 --> 00:16:54,833
Don't you have all this—.

252
00:16:54,833 --> 00:16:55,833
This test solution

253
00:16:56,083 --> 00:16:56,833
was synthesized with whatever

254
00:16:56,834 --> 00:16:58,041
materials I had at the time.

255
00:16:58,458 --> 00:16:59,416
Its efficacy is extremely volatile,

256
00:16:59,417 --> 00:17:00,537
and it cannot be replicated.

257
00:17:00,916 --> 00:17:02,041
The real problem is

258
00:17:02,208 --> 00:17:03,665
I lack a specific antibody

259
00:17:03,666 --> 00:17:04,583
capable of neutralizing

260
00:17:04,583 --> 00:17:05,583
and reversing the virus.

261
00:17:06,458 --> 00:17:07,458
Theoretically,

262
00:17:07,541 --> 00:17:08,749
such an antibody would most likely

263
00:17:08,750 --> 00:17:09,750
exist in that tiny fraction of people

264
00:17:09,751 --> 00:17:10,791
who contracted the virus

265
00:17:11,125 --> 00:17:11,791
yet never turned

266
00:17:11,792 --> 00:17:12,916
into zombies.

267
00:17:17,250 --> 00:17:18,250
I was bitten by a zombie.

268
00:17:18,458 --> 00:17:19,458
And I never mutated.

269
00:17:19,750 --> 00:17:20,666
Could that antibody

270
00:17:20,667 --> 00:17:21,916
be inside my body?

271
00:17:25,125 --> 00:17:26,125
You...

272
00:17:26,333 --> 00:17:27,416
You're an immune subject?

273
00:17:27,708 --> 00:17:28,125
Yes.

274
00:17:28,500 --> 00:17:29,500
I'm immune.

275
00:17:30,125 --> 00:17:31,605
Does that mean you can make the cure?

276
00:17:32,375 --> 00:17:33,375
Yes.

277
00:17:33,625 --> 00:17:34,625
I can.

278
00:17:34,708 --> 00:17:35,708
I know I can.

279
00:17:43,458 --> 00:17:44,708
I'll let you test on me,

280
00:17:45,500 --> 00:17:46,500
but you have to swear

281
00:17:46,958 --> 00:17:48,598
you'll turn my brother back into a human.

282
00:17:49,708 --> 00:17:50,291
Alright.

283
00:17:50,708 --> 00:17:51,708
I swear.

284
00:17:53,791 --> 00:17:54,791
But you must understand,

285
00:17:55,916 --> 00:17:56,916
the testing process

286
00:17:57,291 --> 00:17:58,875
carries unpredictable risks.

287
00:17:59,208 --> 00:18:00,875
It could damage your body,

288
00:18:01,833 --> 00:18:02,833
some of which

289
00:18:03,000 --> 00:18:04,458
might even be irreversible.

290
00:18:05,000 --> 00:18:06,000
Are you sure about this?

291
00:18:09,458 --> 00:18:10,458
I'm sure.

292
00:18:12,083 --> 00:18:12,458
Good.

293
00:18:13,083 --> 00:18:13,625
This matter...

294
00:18:14,083 --> 00:18:15,483
not another soul can ever find out.

295
00:18:17,456 --> 00:18:20,463
The fuel belongs to someone else.
Besides, we're in no rush to leave.

296
00:18:21,166 --> 00:18:22,166
Are we in no rush,

297
00:18:23,166 --> 00:18:24,806
or do you just not want to leave with me?

298
00:18:30,291 --> 00:18:31,583
Is it possible that one day,

299
00:18:32,750 --> 00:18:34,190
zombies could turn back into humans?

300
00:18:34,541 --> 00:18:35,541
A zombie is a zombie.

301
00:18:36,625 --> 00:18:37,625
They can't turn back.

302
00:18:46,875 --> 00:18:47,875
Yeah.
